63,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"@May, C fits the frozen capital line. It avoids per-head licence spikes. The fixed-fee structure prevents the renewal trap I warned about.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"@May, you’re right. My data shows broad tools fail without owners. C is a trap. I support A, the narrow, owned tool that actually survives.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"@Kenji, I understand your point, but A doesn’t stop the copying. C is the only one that fixes the root cause of our daily losses.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,May,,"@Kenji, A is just another task for the same two drowning clerks. If the rota breaks, your app is just expensive noise.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, A is built for offline sync. It works when your network dies. Your staffing crisis doesn’t change physics.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shunichi, offline sync is useful, but the real risk is the renewal notice. We must avoid per-head licence spikes that will shock next year’s budget.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@May, A is for technicians, not clerks. It has a clear owner. My data proves narrow, owned tools survive. C is a broad trap.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,"@May, I worry about the maintenance burden. If C fails, we lose the hour daily. I need to know who owns it before I can support it.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,May,,"@Daisuke, you’re asking the wrong question. It’s not about ownership, it’s about capacity. If the rota breaks, C just becomes another graveyard for your data.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Daisuke, ownership is irrelevant if the signal is dead. A syncs offline; C requires connectivity. Your copying problem is a symptom, not the root cause.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Daisuke, C’s maintenance costs will hit the frozen capital line. A’s fixed-fee structure avoids the renewal trap. I support A for its predictable, sustainable funding model.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Daisuke, C is a graveyard. My data proves broad tools fail without owners. A is narrow, owned, and survives. I vote A.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I’m sorry, but I can’t support A. It ignores the daily copying loss. I vote C, hoping we can find a way to make it work.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,May,,"@Kenji, you’re voting for a tool that ignores the people maintaining it. I vote D, because it automates the handover notes drowning my clerks.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, D is useless if the network is down. A works offline. I vote A.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,I vote A. Its fixed-fee structure avoids the renewal trap. Predictable costs are essential given the frozen capital line.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Kenji,,"I vote A. My data proves narrow, owned tools survive. C is a broad trap. Let’s fund the solution that actually sticks.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
